\name{sdrf_validate}
\alias{sdrf_validate}
\title{Check one SDRF file against the specification's structural rules}
\usage{
sdrf_validate(path, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a \code{.sdrf.tsv} file.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Check SDRF-Proteomics files against the specification's structural rules, one row per finding.

Calls mzLib's \code{SdrfValidator.Validate}. Structure only: required and recommended columns,
column order, casing, malformed names, ragged rows, integer replicate and fraction columns,
reserved-word casing, and the one hard row rule - \code{source name} + \code{assay name} +
the \code{comment} column for the label must be unique. Controlled-vocabulary accessions are \strong{not}
resolved.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf_validation}: \code{path}, \code{is_valid}, and the counts - \code{error_count} and
  \code{warning_count} findings of each severity, \code{message_count} findings in all, \code{row_count} data
  rows in the document (the header is not a row) - plus \code{caveats}, \code{column_names}, and
  \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per finding: \code{severity} (\code{"Error"} or \code{"Warning"}),
  \code{rule} (a stable name such as \code{"RequiredColumn"}), \code{message}, \code{row_index} (the data row,
  1-based), \code{line_number} (the file line, 1-based, the header being line 1) and
  \code{column_name}. \code{row_index} and \code{line_number} are \code{NA} for a finding about the whole
  document; \code{column_name} is \code{NA} when the finding is not about one column.

}
\section{Warnings never make a document invalid}{


\code{is_valid} is \code{TRUE} whenever there is no \code{Error}. mzLib calibrated every severity against the
1,236-file curated corpus, and a rule that fired on most curated files was judged wrong rather
than the files. And a valid document may still say nothing about its samples: that is what
\code{\link{sdrf_assess}} asks.

}
